{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>After the doings of the land of Egypt, wherein you dwelt, etc.</b> He employed ״אֲשֶׁר יְשַׁבְתֶּם בָּהּ״ and ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מֵבִיא אֶתְכֶם שָׁמָּה״ as an extension — for presumably Yosef selected for his brothers and for his father's house a place that was not so corrupt, so that the coming generations would not learn from their deeds. And likewise, presumably the Holy One, blessed be He, selected for Israel a place that was not so corrupt, so that they would not learn from them — for behold it is written (Exodus 23:30) ״מְעַט מְעַט אֲגָרְשֶׁנּוּ מִפָּנֶיךָ״, and before He drives them all out there is reason to worry lest they learn from their deeds. Therefore He said that even their doings you shall not do, and all the more so the other abominations of Egypt among whom you did not dwell, and all the more so the other nations. But according to the explanation of Rashi, who explained the precise opposite of this, it is difficult: is it conceivable to say that specifically those among whom they dwelt and those to whom I am bringing you there — the most corrupt of all — you shall not do, but the doings of those who are not so corrupt you may do? Heaven forbid to say so! Rather, he employed it as an extension, and so that Israel would not have room to err concerning them and say: since they were settled in the land of Goshen, surely the reason is because their deeds are not corrupt at all; and likewise, since the Holy One, blessed be He, is bringing us to this land, surely their deeds are not corrupt — and the reason they were driven from their land is because they dwelt in a land not theirs, for behold the Canaanite had gone and conquered the land from the seed of Shem. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to make known that their deeds too are corrupt, and even if not as corrupt as their fellows, nevertheless there is enough disgrace and abhorrence in their deeds, and all the more so regarding their fellows." "And since He did not combine them but said ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ״ twice, we may say that He specified each sin separately, and Scripture is indicting Israel for having sought to establish permanent settlement in Egypt, as I explained above at the end of parashat Vayigash (Genesis 47:27) on the verse ״וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּאֶרֶץ גּוֹשֶׁן״ — it speaks of Israel's guilt, and Elohim held them guilty for seeking permanent settlement in a place where Elohim had told them they would be sojourners there and not residents. And all this they did because they were entrenched in the idols of Egypt, as it is written in Ezekiel (20:5–8): ״וָאִוָּדַע לָהֶם בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם וְגוֹ׳ וַיַּמְרוּ בִי וְגוֹ׳ וְאֶת גִּלּוּלֵי מִצְרַיִם לֹא עָזָבוּ״, etc. And so Rashi explained in parashat Bo (Exodus 10:23) that in that generation there were wicked people who did not want to leave Egypt and died in the three days of darkness. And concerning this it is said here ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ — that same deed which you did in the land of Egypt; and what is the evil deed which you did? — ״אֲשֶׁר יְשַׁבְתֶּם בָּהּ״, that you sought permanent settlement there because your soul craved their idols and their soul desired their abominations. ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ״ — you shall not do such things again, to seek permanent settlement among a rebellious people who walk in a way that is not good, lest you learn from their deeds — for so it happened to you in Egypt." "And ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן״ — this means that He is indicting Israel for having despised the Holy Land, which was beloved to the patriarchs, while they despised it, to the point that the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to bring them there against their will, without their consent. And this is what He said: ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן״ — that same deed which you did in the land of Canaan; and what is the deed? — ״אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי מֵבִיא אֶתְכֶם שָׁמָּה״, for your eyes see that I am bringing you there against your will. For in Elim they said (Exodus 16:3): ״מִי יִתֵּן מוּתֵנוּ בְּיַד ה׳ בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״; the spies said (Numbers 14:4): ״נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״; and likewise our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 21:10): the women cherished the land and the men despised it; and likewise the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to divert them through the wilderness for forty years so they would not return to Egypt. And from all this there is proof that they despised the Holy Land, chosen since the days of the patriarchs as a place of holiness and purity — and most of the commandments of the Torah depend on the land — and in all this Israel showed contempt, to the point that the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to bring them there against their will and to carry them on eagles' wings, as a nurse carries the suckling to that place, and by way of circuiting through deserts so they would have no place to turn back. ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ כֵן עוֹד״ — you shall not do so again, but you shall go willingly to the place which HaElohim said, for this is what is prepared for keeping all the mishpatim of YHWH and His chukkim. And He gave a reason for each one individually: concerning what He said, that they should not seek permanent settlement in an impure land in Egypt, so that they would not learn to do their abominations — concerning this He said ״וּבְחֻקֹּתֵיהֶם לֹא תֵלֵכוּ״. And concerning the latter matter, that He urged them to go to the Holy Land in order to fulfill the commandments that depend on the land — concerning this He said ״אֶת מִשְׁפָּטַי תַּעֲשׂוּ״ etc., for they are the mishpatim of the Elohim of the land."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:25.084064073Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}